        "Modern planning descends from the industrial city's reformers: Howard's garden city of 1898 proposed limited-size towns ringed by greenbelt, realised at Letchworth and Welwyn, and Perry's neighbourhood unit organised housing around a school and shops. Abuja's master plan borrowed the garden city and neighbourhood unit vocabulary openly. In {S}, knowing this lineage explains why Nigerian plans carry green belts, neighbourhood centres and ordered hierarchies.",
        "Planning theory divides on how decisions should be made: the rational comprehensive model surveys all options and optimises; Lindblom's incrementalism muddles through with successive limited comparisons; advocacy planning, from Paul Davidoff, argues plural plans for contending interests; communicative theory stresses negotiation. In {S}, real plans mix all four, and examiners expect you to name the tension between them.",
        "Regional planning coordinates development beyond single towns: growth pole theory, from Perroux, seeds propulsive industries to pull a region upward; corridor plans string development along transport spines; river basin authorities manage water across states. Nigeria's regional instruments include the Niger Delta development commission and the river basin authorities. In {S}, the regional scale is where infrastructure, not plot layout, does the heavy lifting.",
        "The Nigerian Urban and Regional Planning Act, Decree 88 of 1992, now Cap N138, LFN 2004, is the country's umbrella planning law. It creates planning boards and authorities at federal, state and local tiers, requires master plans for planning areas, subjects development to permission, and provides for enforcement, compensation and appeals. In {S}, it supersedes the older town and country planning ordinances and anchors every development control decision.",
        "Development control tests proposals against approved plans and standards: permitted uses, plot coverage, plot ratio, setbacks, building lines, parking and access. Permission is applied for, screened, sometimes publicised, then granted with conditions or refused with reasons, and conditions bind successors in title. In {S}, control is where policy meets a drawing set, and consistency is the whole reputation of the office.",
        "The Abuja master plan of 1979, prepared by International Planning Associates, organised the Federal Capital Territory into phases, districts and a central area with greenbelts and satellite towns; implementation strains came from population overshoot and informal growth. Lagos's 1980 master plan and later sub-regional plans guided a metropolis that outgrew its documents. In {S}, both teach that plans need review cycles and honest data.",
        "Zoning divides planning areas into use zones with density controls: plot ratio, total floor area over site area, caps building bulk, plot coverage caps the footprint, and setbacks protect light and air. High-plot-ratio districts grow towers; low-ratio districts keep low skylines. In {S}, a plot ratio of 2 means total floor area twice the plot, the single number that shapes a skyline.",
        "Subdivision control converts raw land into serviced layouts: access roads, drainage, water and power reserves, open space, and plots of lawful size set by standards. The planning system requires layout approval before plots can be sold or built on, protecting buyers from unserviceable paper estates. In {S}, checking a layout against standards is a routine professional task with legal force.",
        "Institutions tier by scale: the federal level sets policy and handles the Federal Capital Territory and interstate matters, state planning boards prepare and approve plans for their areas, and local planning authorities process development permits in their districts. Registration of town planners is by TOPREC, with NITP as the professional body. In {S}, knowing which tier decides what saves applicants months of misdirected paperwork.",
